  "id": "COMPOUND:18406 - PROTEIN:1442",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence strongly suggests a mechanistic link between the compound [(3S,8S,9S,10R,13S,14S,16S,17R)-17-acetyl-3-[(2R,3R,4S,5R,6R)-4-[(2S,3R,4S,5R,6S)-3,4-dihydroxy-6-methyl-5-[(2S,3R,4S,5S,6R)-3,4,5-trihydroxy-6-(hydroxymethyl)oxan-2-yl]oxyoxan-2-yl]oxy-5-hydroxy-6-(hydroxymethyl)-3-[(2S,3R,4R,5R,6S)-3,4,5-trihydroxy-6-methyloxan-2-yl]oxyoxan-2-yl]oxy-10,13-dimethyl-2,3,4,7,8,9,11,12,14,15,16,17-dodecahydro-1H-cyclopenta[a]phenanthren-16-yl] (4S)-4-methyl-5-[(2S,3R,4S,5S,6R)-3,4,5-trihydroxy-6-(hydroxymethyl)oxan-2-yl]oxypentanoate and DNA polymerase alpha subunit B. This is supported by multiple independent pathways, all of which point to the compound as a target of multiple compounds that are known to interact with DNA polymerase alpha subunit B. These compounds, including Stemanthrene D, 3R,5S-Sonnerlactone, and Pityriarubin B, are all known to inhibit DNA polymerase alpha subunit B, suggesting a common mechanism of action involving the enzyme.\n\nThe evidence also indicates that the compound may act through a mechanism that involves the tumor necrosis factor receptor superfamily member 10B, which is a known target of these compounds. This suggests that the compound may interact with the tumor necrosis factor receptor superfamily member 10B, potentially modulating its function and leading to inhibition of DNA polymerase alpha subunit B. The presence of multiple independent pathways and the consistent targeting of DNA polymerase alpha subunit B across different compounds supports the hypothesis that the compound may have a direct or indirect effect on the enzyme.\n\nThe mechanism of action is not fully elucidated, but the evidence suggests that the compound may interact with DNA polymerase alpha subunit B through a pathway involving the tumor necrosis factor receptor superfamily member 10B. This provides a plausible mechanism for the observed inhibition of DNA polymerase alpha subunit B, although further experimental validation is needed to confirm the exact interaction and the precise molecular mechanism.",
